# FEATURE-LEGACY-RME-ODONTOGRAM-MASS-UPLOAD-1 — durable rules

## 1. Mass upload is ORCHESTRATION over single-item truth

Every clinical decision belongs to the canonical single-item service. Mass upload
owns batching, package safety, preflight reporting and bounded dispatch — nothing
else.

Never reimplement inside `MassUpload/`: date bounds, branch resolution or
admission, patient resolution, the single-active-document rule, source validation,
SOD, publish, VOID, or history. If a rule needs to exist in both places, it
belongs in the canonical service and is *read* here.

Lifecycles are created **only** by `LegacyRmeImportService::createFromUpload()` /
`LegacyOdontogramImportService::createFromUpload()`.

## 2. MASS UPLOAD ≠ MASS PUBLISH

Mass upload may ingest, validate, stage, enqueue, process and prepare review. It
must never publish. Maker–checker, the separate publisher, and VOID authority are
untouched. A mass uploader must not gain `publish_*`, `review_*` or `void_*`.

## 3. No new permission, ever

Mass RME reuses `create_legacy_rme_imports`. Mass odontogram reuses
`create_legacy_odontogram_imports`. A `mass_upload_*` permission is forbidden —
a role grant added to make a menu appear is how privilege creeps. A test asserts
no permission name contains `mass_upload` or `legacy_mass`.

## 4. No second locking system

§15's "re-read occupancy under a lock" already happens inside
`createFromUpload()`, which takes the PostgreSQL advisory lock. The mass layer
must take **no** patient lock: two lock orders over the same rows is how
deadlocks are built.

Consequence, proven by mutation testing: removing *both* mass-layer idempotency
guards still produces no duplicate, because the canonical slot guard refuses it.
Those guards exist to prevent the pointless **attempt** (stored file to
compensate, refusal audit, wasted render capacity) — not the duplicate. Assert
them against the audit trail, not against a duplicate count.

## 5. A stale preflight is a BLOCKED item, not an error

Preflight is a forecast. Between preflight and creation a single upload may
publish, so `createFromUpload()` re-decides and refuses. Record that as BLOCKED
with the precise occupancy reason — resolve it by asking
`previewForNewLifecycle()` again, never by parsing a validation message.

## 6. Blocked items never enqueue

Only statuses in `LegacyMassUploadItemStatus::dispatchable()` are ever created.
BLOCKED is not among them and a confirmation cannot promote it. The confirm form
carries no row selection by design.

## 7. RME and Odontogram are independent — and asymmetric

Separate slots, separate advisory-lock keys, separate manifests. A published
legacy RME must never block a first legacy chart.

Do **not** invent symmetry. RME has a source-RM binding, a date RANGE and an
origin branch; odontogram has a single date and no wave/admission service. Mirror
neither onto the other.

## 8. The odontogram wrong-patient gate is load-bearing

`LegacyOdontogramImportService` takes no `sourceRm`, so single odontogram upload's
only wrong-patient defence is a human checkbox — which mass upload removes. The
mass odontogram adapter therefore asserts the manifest RM against the resolved
patient via `LegacyRmeSourcePatientBindingService::bind()`, at **preflight and
again on the write path**, and hard-blocks any mismatch.

The write-path assertion is NOT redundant: unlike every other gate it is not
re-evaluated inside the canonical call. Removing it is a wrong-patient
regression, not a cleanup.

## 9. The manifest is never trusted for identity or authorization

Link patients by **medical record number**, resolved server-side, exactly one
match or the row is blocked. `nik`, `ktp`, `patient_id` and `branch_id` are
refused **by column name** — the first is privacy, the latter two are spoofing
surfaces. Branch always comes from the patient and `BranchContext`.

A partial RM must be refused (`SOURCE_RM_INVALID`), because patient lookup falls
back to a suffix match across all branches and at bulk scale that is a
wrong-patient generator.

## 10. One patient, one row, one document per manifest

A patient appearing twice is blocked (`DUPLICATE_MANIFEST_PATIENT`) — only
preflight can see this, since at that point neither occurrence has created
anything. Multi-year paper archives are merged into ONE document with
earliest/latest dates, never submitted as several lifecycles.

## 11. Package safety fails CLOSED and structurally

`ZipArchive::extractTo()` is forbidden. Stream each entry to a filename **we**
generate; the archive's name is only ever a manifest key. That makes Zip Slip
impossible rather than merely sanitised.

Still reject unsafe archives (traversal, absolute paths, backslashes, symlinks,
duplicate leaf names, bombs, non-PDF bytes). Check the decompressed total twice —
a crafted central directory can understate its payload.

## 12. Human date verification, no OCR authority

Dates come from the operator's manifest. Nothing reads a date out of a PDF.

## 13. No new queue, no new daily business quota

Mass upload reuses the existing consumed queues (`legacy-rme-documents`,
`legacy-odontogram-documents`) via the canonical jobs. Do not add a third.

The hub daily quota is currently `null` (unlimited) and mass upload adds no cap
of its own — it honours `preview()`/`remainingToday()` so a future declared limit
surfaces in preflight. Technical backpressure (chunked dispatch) is not a quota.

## 14. Cancellation is a staging operation only

Cancel is available only before any lifecycle exists. Withdrawing a real document
is VOID, on that document, under its own authority. Cancel must never become a
clinical delete.

## 15. Private storage, deterministic cleanup

ZIP, manifest and extracted PDFs live on `legacy_mass_upload_private`. Never a
public disk, never the webroot. The workspace is removed on success, on package
rejection, on cancel and on exception — but never a document the canonical
service already owns, which is source evidence under clinical retention.

## 16. Reports and audit carry no identity

The downloadable report and every audit payload may carry row numbers, the
operator's own RM, reason codes, digests and counts. Never a patient name, NIK,
date of birth, clinical content, absolute path or exception text. CSV cells are
escaped against formula injection.

## 17. Route names stay disjoint from single upload

`settings.rme.legacy-mass-imports.*` and
`settings.rme.legacy-mass-odontograms.*`. Naming them under
`legacy-imports.*` would make the SINGLE upload sidebar entry light up on mass
pages, because that entry matches `routeIs('settings.rme.legacy-imports.*')`.

## 18. Sidebar is not a boundary

Menu entries are gated by the same permission AND the same feature guard as their
single-upload siblings. Every route is independently gated server-side, and a
batch of the wrong type 404s on the other surface.

## 19. Single upload is never removed

It remains the right tool for one-patient corrections, VOID replacement, retry
and small manual migrations.

## 20. CI: the gate is a name allowlist

`LegacyRme` does **not** match `LegacyMassUploadRmeTest`. The `LegacyMassUpload`
token must stay in both critical-gate variants, and the three mandatory suites
stay declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.

## 21. The APPLICATION package limit is canonical; the runtime sits above it

BUGFIX-MASS-UPLOAD-REQUEST-ENTITY-TOO-LARGE-1.

`config/legacy_mass_upload.php` → `package.max_bytes` is the ONLY authority on
how large a package may be. nginx and PHP-FPM exist to let a package the
application would accept actually arrive, and to let a package the application
would refuse arrive anyway so the operator reads a reason instead of a status
code.

Every transport ceiling must therefore sit at or ABOVE `package.max_bytes`:

    nginx  client_max_body_size      >= package.max_bytes   (and > it, for
                                        multipart overhead)
    FPM    upload_max_filesize       >= package.max_bytes
    FPM    post_max_size             >  upload_max_filesize

Pin `client_max_body_size` to the SAME value as `post_max_size`, so nginx is
always the outer bound. If PHP is the one that refuses an oversize POST it
discards `$_POST` entirely, the CSRF token goes with it, and the operator is
told "Page Expired" (419) — a message about sessions for a problem about size.

A 413 is always an infrastructure defect, never a product decision. Diagnose it
by naming the layer: `nginx -T | grep -c client_max_body_size` returning 0 means
the COMPILED DEFAULT of 1 MiB is in force, which is what refused a 2 860 825-byte
package in production on 2026-10-03 — and, with the same default, a 1 189 484-byte
single-item legacy import and a 5 333 569-byte lab workflow request before it.
The request never reaches PHP or Laravel, so no application log and no
application test can see it.

Never fix only nginx. The pool inherits php.ini, and stock php.ini is
`upload_max_filesize = 2M` / `post_max_size = 8M`; raising nginx alone moves the
failure one layer down, where PHP silently drops the file and Laravel reports the
misleading "Paket arsip ZIP wajib diunggah."

## 22. Transport ceilings are bounded, and independent of archive safety

`client_max_body_size 0` (unlimited) is forbidden. So is `memory_limit = -1`.
These are clinical documents; an unbounded archive upload is a disk-exhaustion
and worker-exhaustion surface.

Raising a TRANSPORT ceiling must never raise an ARCHIVE-SAFETY ceiling. They are
separate controls and are asserted separately: `document_max_bytes` (20 MiB),
`manifest_max_bytes` (5 MiB), `max_entries` (1200),
`total_uncompressed_max_bytes` (2 GiB), `max_compression_ratio` (120.0). The bomb
guard, the decompressed-total guard, the per-item guard and the Zip Slip,
symlink and magic-byte guards are unaffected by anything in this section.

Scope the relaxation as narrowly as the mechanism allows. `max_execution_time` is
`PHP_INI_ALL`, so the intake route adjusts it for ITSELF
(`legacy_mass_upload.intake.max_execution_seconds`) and every other request on
the pool keeps the 30s ceiling. `upload_max_filesize`, `post_max_size` and
`max_input_time` are `PHP_INI_PERDIR` and cannot be set per route, which is the
only reason they live at pool level. A configured intake budget of 0 means
"no limit" to PHP and is ignored rather than honoured.

**`set_time_limit()` RAISES ONLY — it never lowers, and never imposes a limit
where there is none.** `set_time_limit()` does not extend a budget; it REPLACES
it and restarts the counter. So read `ini_get('max_execution_time')` first and
return early when it is `0` (unlimited) or already at least as generous.

This is not hypothetical. The first version of the intake budget called
`set_time_limit(900)` unconditionally, and because the **CLI SAPI defaults
`max_execution_time` to 0**, one feature test POSTing to the route armed a
900-second kill timer on the entire Pest process. CI died with
`Pest\Exceptions\FatalException: Maximum execution time of 900 seconds
exceeded` — in an unrelated suite, long after the upload test had passed. Every
local suite finished inside 900s (the longest was 731s), so nothing local could
show it. Any new `set_time_limit()` anywhere in this codebase must carry the same
two guards, and a long-lived process (`queue:work`, `artisan test`, a scheduler
tick) is exactly what they protect.

## 23. Runtime limits are config-as-code, not a hand edit in /etc

`deploy/php-fpm/daengtisiams.conf` is the version-controlled source of the
production pool and is expected to match it byte-for-byte — verify with
`sha256sum`. Declare the ceilings there, with `php_admin_value`, so they survive
a php.ini change and are reviewable. Do not rely on inheriting php.ini: this
project's php.ini was never tuned, and the pool header asserted the opposite for
months.

Production nginx is NOT deployed from this repository, so
`deploy/nginx/upload-body-size.conf` is the version-controlled record and must
carry its own one-time apply and verify instructions (the
`deploy/nginx/pwa-manifest-mime.conf` pattern). Scope it to the DaengtisiaMS
`server` block by `include`, never to `http` — the catch-all `default_server`,
the redirect blocks and any co-tenant virtual host on this shared VPS keep
nginx's 1 MiB default. A one-off manual VPS tweak that a future deploy can erase
is not a fix.
